,"to_t":2000.4071038251366,"title":"President of Fiji","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Kamisese Mara"},{"qid":"Q712","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q5541411","value_label":"Jioji Konrote","from_t":2015.86301369863,"to_t":2021.86301369863,"title":"President of Fiji","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Jioji Konrote"},{"qid":"Q712","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q57830","value_label":"Frank Bainimarama","from_t":2000.4071038251366,"to_t":2000.5300546448088,"title":"President of Fiji","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Frank Bainimarama"},{"qid":"Q712","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q57830","value_label":"Frank Bainimarama","from_t":2006.9260273972602,"to_t":2007.0082191780823,"title":"President of Fiji","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Frank Bainimarama"},{"qid":"Q712","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q6959729","value_label":"Naiqama Lalabalavu","from_t":2024.863387978142,"to_t":null,"title":"President of Fiji","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Naiqama Lalabalavu"},{"qid":"Q712","kind":"flag","value_qid":"Q182514","value_label":"Flag of Fiji.svg","from_t":1970.772602739726,"to_t":null,"title":"flag of Fiji","property":"P163","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"flag of Fiji"}],"people":{"Q109012733":{"label":"Wiliame Katonivere","description":"6th President of Fiji (2021-2024)","enwiki":"Wiliame Katonivere","summary":"Ratu Wiliame Maivalili Katonivere, CF (pronounced [ɰiliame mɛiβalili katoniβere]; born 20 April 1964) is a Fijian chief and politician who served as the President of Fiji from 2021 to 2024. He has been chief of Macuata Province since 2013, succeeding his older brother Aisea Katonivere; he was previously involved in conservation initiatives of Fiji's Great Sea Reef.\nKatonivere has been the chairman of the Pine Group of Companies from 2020 to 2021, which includes Fiji Pine Limited, Tropik Wood Industries Limited and Tropik Wood Products Limited. He is also a board member for Fiji Airports, Fiji Sugar Corporation and Rewa Rice Ltd.","image":"Wiliame Katonivere 2022.jpg","birth":1964.3005464480875,"death":null},"Q981549":{"label":"Sitiveni Rabuka","description":"Prime Minister of Fiji from 1992 to 1999 and from 2022-","enwiki":"Sitiveni Rabuka","summary":"Sitiveni Ligamamada Rabuka  (Fijian: [sitʃiˈβeni ramˈbuka]; born 13 September 1948) is a Fijian politician, sportsman, and former soldier who has been serving as Prime Minister of Fiji since 24 December 2022. He was the instigator of two military coups in 1987. He was democratically elected as Prime Minister of Fiji, serving from 1992 to 1999, and again in 2022, leading a three-party coalition. He also served as Chairman of the Great Council of Chiefs from 1999 to 2001, and later as Chairman of the Cakaudrove Provincial Council from 2001 to 2008.","image":"2025 Sitiveni Rabuka (cropped).jpg","birth":1948.6994535519125,"death":null},"Q1398207":{"label":"Penaia Ganilau","description":"President of Fiji and Governor-General of Fiji (1918-1993)","enwiki":"Penaia Ganilau","summary":"Ratu Sir Penaia Kanatabatu Ganilau  (28 July 1918 – 15 December 1993) was a Fijian politician who served as the first President of Fiji, serving from 8 December 1987 until his death in 1993. He had previously served as Governor-General of Fiji, representing Elizabeth II, Queen of Fiji, from 12 February 1983 to 15 October 1987.","image":"Penaia Ganilau 1961.jpg","birth":1918.5698630136985,"death":1993.9534246575342},"Q316317":{"label":"Josefa Iloilo","description":"President of Fiji from 2000 to 2009 (1920–2011)","enwiki":"Josefa Iloilo","summary":"Ratu Josefa Iloilovatu Uluivuda,  (29 December 1920 – 6 February 2011) was a Fijian politician who served as the 3rd President of Fiji from 2000 until 2009 following the 2000 Fijian coup d'état, excluding a brief period from 5 December 2006 to 4 January 2007 amid the 2006 coup d'état. He held the traditional title of Tui Vuda, the paramount chief of the Vuda district in Ba Province on Fiji's northwest coast. Like many Fijian people, he rarely used his surname and was known simply as Josefa Iloilo. He announced on 28 July 2009 that he would be leaving office on 30 July.","image":"Josefa Iloilo.jpg","birth":1920.9918032786886,"death":2011.0986301369862},"Q373605":{"label":"Epeli Nailatikau","description":"President of Fiji from 2009 to 2015 (1941–2026)","enwiki":"Epeli Nailatikau","summary":"Brigadier-General Ratu Epeli Nailatikau,  (Fijian pronunciation: [epeli nailatʃikau]; 5 July 1941 – 26 March 2026), often referred to as Na Turaga Mai Naisogolaca, was a Fijian chief who was President of Fiji from 2009 to 2015. He had a long career in the Military, diplomatic service, and government. From 2001 to 2006 he served as Speaker of the House of Representatives – the lower and more powerful chamber of the Fijian Parliament. He was also the chairman of the Parliamentary Appropriations Committee and of the House Committee.","image":"Epeli Nailatikau 2012.jpg","birth":1941.5068493150684,"death":2026.2301369863014},"Q469507":{"label":"Kamisese Mara","description":"President of Fiji (1920-2004)","enwiki":"Kamisese Mara","summary":"Ratu Sir Kamisese Mara,  (6 May 1920 – 18 April 2004) was a Fijian politician who served as Chief Minister from 1967 to 1970, when Fiji gained its independence from the United Kingdom, and, apart from one brief interruption in 1987, as the first Prime Minister from 1970 to 1992. He subsequently served as president from 1993 to 2000.","image":"Kamisese Mara.jpg","birth":1920.344262295082,"death":2004.295081967213},"Q5541411":{"label":"Jioji Konrote","description":"Fijian politician (born 1947)","enwiki":"Jioji Konrote","summary":"Major-General Jioji \"George\" Konousi Konrote,  (born 26 December 1947) is a Fijian politician and retired Major-General of the Fiji Military who served as the President of Fiji from 2015 to 2021. After commanding a peacekeeping mission in Lebanon, Konrote served as Fiji's High Commissioner to Australia from 2001 to 2006, as Minister of State for Immigration briefly in 2006, and as Minister for Employment Opportunities, Productivity and Industrial Relations from 2014 to 2015. He was the first non-iTaukei president (as he is Rotuman), the first not to be a chief, and the first Seventh-day Adventist to be elected by parliament, as previous presidents were selected by the Great Council of Chiefs.","image":"Jioji Konrote December 2017.jpg","birth":1947.9835616438356,"death":null},"Q57830":{"label":"Frank Bainimarama","description":"Prime Minister of Fiji from 2007 to 2022 (born 1954)","enwiki":"Frank Bainimarama","summary":"Josaia Voreqe \"Frank\" Bainimarama  (Fijian pronunciation: [tʃoˈsɛia βoˈreŋɡe mbɛiniˈmarama]; born 27 April 1954) is a Fijian former politician and naval officer who served as the prime minister of Fiji from 2007 until 2022. A member of the FijiFirst party, which he founded in 2014, he began his career as an officer in the Fijian navy and commander of the Fijian military.\nDespite being suspended from Parliament, he served as the opposition leader from 24 December 2022 until 8 March 2023, when he resigned and was replaced by Inia Seruiratu.\nBainimarama attended Marist Brothers High School, the Asian Institute of Technology and Dalhousie University.","image":"Frank Bainimarama September 2014.jpg","birth":1954.317808219178,"death":null},"Q6959729":{"label":"Naiqama Lalabalavu","description":"Fijian politician","enwiki":"Naiqama Lalabalavu","summary":"Ratu Naiqama Tawakecolati Lalabalavu  (Fijian pronunciation: [naiŋɡama taɰakeðolatʃi lalambalaβu]; born 23 December 1953) is a Fijian Paramount Chief and the current President of Fiji. He has served as the leader of the opposition and as Speaker of the Parliament of Fiji.\nHe was appointed a Member of the Order of the British Empire (MBE) in the 1981 Birthday Honours for public and community service.\nOn 31 October 2024 he was elected President of Fiji; he took office on 12 November 2024.","image":"Naiqama Lalabalavu January 2026.jpg","birth":1953.9753424657533,"death":null}},"media":{"Flag of Fiji.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Flag_of_Fiji.svg/330px-Flag_of_Fiji.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Fiji.svg","author":"Tessa Mackenzie","license":"Public domain"},"Coat of arms of Fiji.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Coat_of_arms_of_Fiji.svg/330px-Coat_of_arms_of_Fiji.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_Fiji.svg","author":"Simi Tukidia","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},"Wiliame Katonivere 2022.jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Wiliame_Katonivere_2022.jpg/330px-Wiliame_Katonivere_2022.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wiliame_Katonivere_2022.jpg","author":"US Embassy Suva","license":"Public domain"},"2025 Sitiveni Rabuka (cropped).jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/2025_Sitiveni_Rabuka_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-2025_Sitiveni_Rabuka_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2025_Sitiveni_Rabuka_(cropped).jpg","author":"Prime Minister's Office","license":"GODL-India"},"Penaia Ganilau 1961.jpg":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/64/Penaia_Ganilau_1961.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Penaia_Ganilau_1961.jpg","author":"Unknown authorUnknown author","license":"Public domain"},"Josefa Iloilo.jpg":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c2/Josefa_Iloilo.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_cam